WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
JIAHAO LI: My name is Jiahao Li.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.800
And my SuperUROP project is
about making your program run

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.150
faster.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.890
So nowadays, computer systems,
especially computation

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.549
dense ones, have more
than one CPU cores.

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:13.590
So people need to write
parallel program in order

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.450
to utilize all those
cores simultaneously.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.480
However, the compiler
we have today

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.520
is not very effective
at optimizing

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.150
these parallel programs.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:24.780
So my project aims
to change that.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.370
And our primary
approach actually

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.560
stems from this
observation that there

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.009
are a lot of optimizations that
our compiler does today that

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.380
work perfectly on
serial programs

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.870
but fail to translate
to parallel programs,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.960
because of the unfamiliar
parallel constructs that can

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.830
be found in parallel programs.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.060
So in order to address
this, we actually

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.760
drew our inspiration from a
prior project called Tapir.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.830
So when we have a
parallel program,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.950
we create a mirror
program of it that does

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.090
the exact same computation.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.330
But we get rid of all
the parallel constructs.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.870
We call it the serial projection
of this parallel program.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.000
We then run the
compiler optimization

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.650
on the serial projection.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.710
And for each transformation
the optimization does,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.720
we mirror it back onto the
parallel program, the one

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.490
we want to optimize.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.660
So by doing this in the
evaluation that we have run,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.410
we have been able to observe
of a speed-up of up to 31%

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.100
on certain benchmarks.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.000
So this means the program would
take 31% less time to run.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.510
So I think with this we have
demonstrated that is, indeed,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.985
possible and feasible
to effectively implement

00:01:29.985 --> 00:01:33.720
the optimization for parallel
programs in a compiler.

